<h2 id="design-principles">1.2 Design principles</h2>
<ul>
<li><p>Never bind a bulk job to an ICN browser session or servlet
thread.</p></li>
<li><p>FileNet BulkRequest objects are the durable source of truth for
submission, status, recovery and audit.</p></li>
<li><p>Every side effect must be idempotent or protected by a compact
batch checkpoint.</p></li>
<li><p>Authorization is checked at submission and revalidated at
execution.</p></li>
<li><p>One failing item must not necessarily fail a 100,000-item
request.</p></li>
<li><p>Repository protection wins over maximum throughput: bounded
concurrency, rate limits and back-pressure.</p></li>
<li><p>Progress, errors and audit evidence must be understandable to
users and support teams.</p></li>
</ul>
<h1 id="scope-and-assumptions">2. Scope and assumptions</h1>
<h2 id="in-scope">2.1 In scope</h2>
<ul>
<li><p>ICN context actions from Browse and Search for eligible documents
and folders.</p></li>
<li><p>Copy, move, delete and restore bulk requests.</p></li>
<li><p>Recursive folder traversal with configurable limits.</p></li>
<li><p>Dedicated My Requests / All Requests dashboard.</p></li>
<li><p>Progress, details, cancellation, retry, downloadable error report
and notifications.</p></li>
<li><p>Admin configuration, security, audit, health metrics and
operational recovery.</p></li>
</ul>
<h2 id="out-of-scope-for-release-1">2.2 Out of scope for release 1</h2>
<ul>
<li><p>Cross-object-store copy/move unless a separate migration mapping
is defined.</p></li>
<li><p>Content transformation, metadata remapping or business workflow
initiation.</p></li>
<li><p>Transactional rollback of an entire multi-thousand-item
request.</p></li>
<li><p>Guaranteeing an exact preview count while the repository is
actively changing.</p></li>
</ul>
