<h2 id="why-not-a-raw-java-thread">3.1 Why not a raw Java thread?</h2>
<p>A raw thread created by the REST endpoint is owned by that JVM. A
restart, deployment, out-of-memory event or node failure can terminate
it. Use a bounded managed executor for immediate processing, while the
FileNet request remains recoverable. A scheduler periodically queries
PENDING, QUEUED and stale RUNNING requests and submits them to the
managed executor after a successful claim.</p>
<h2 id="submission-and-recovery-consistency">3.2 Submission and recovery
consistency</h2>
<p>ICN first creates the FileNet request in PENDING and calls Spring
Boot with its object ID and an idempotency key. Spring Boot validates
and changes it to QUEUED, submits it to the managed executor, and
returns 202 Accepted. If the JVM fails anywhere in this sequence, the
recovery scheduler discovers the durable FileNet object and completes
dispatch. Duplicate calls return or reuse the same request rather than
creating duplicate work.</p>
