<h1 id="final-recommendation">20. Final recommendation</h1>
<p>Proceed with BulkRequest custom objects and a custom ICN dashboard.
Use FileNet as the durable request store and lightweight job queue,
backed by a managed Spring Boot executor, FileNet-based
leases/heartbeats, compact batch checkpoints and a recovery scheduler.
Start with Copy as the reference implementation, prove restart safety
and repository guardrails, then add Move, Delete and Restore once their
exact FileNet semantics and security rules are signed off.</p>
<p>This design removes browser/session dependency while preserving user
visibility, operational control and FileNet governance. It is
appropriate for very large folders only when capacity limits,
back-pressure and production benchmarks are part of the release—not
optional follow-up work.</p>
<h2 id="when-an-external-database-may-be-reconsidered">20.1 When an
external database may be reconsidered</h2>
<p>A separate application database is not part of the baseline.
Reconsider it only if production evidence shows that FileNet polling or
progress/checkpoint writes create unacceptable load, if millions of
per-item outcomes must be queried, if complex cross-node scheduling is
required, or if exact item-level resume cannot be implemented safely
with compact FileNet checkpoints. It should be an optimization driven by
measured limits, not a prerequisite.</p>
